          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "bb16f798-0cac-4822-92c2-2f0da762f76e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In the provided code snippet, the function `fprintf` is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fprintf`, consider using `fputs` or `fwrite` which do not interpret the format string. Always validate and sanitize input data. Avoid using functions that do not check bounds or that allow direct memory access.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nchar error_message[50];\nint written = snprintf(error_message, sizeof(error_message), \"[ERROR]: Invalid register space named <%d>\\n\", reg_type);\nif (written > 0 && written < sizeof(error_message)) {\n    fputs(error_message, stderr);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `stdio.h`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f5d99ff3-dbc2-4cd3-a7c1-93deadf7dccd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often referred to as \"banned\" or \"prohibited\" functions. The `ctime` function is one such function. It converts a time_t value into a string, but it uses a static buffer that can be overwritten by subsequent calls, leading to potential data corruption or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `ctime` function. Instead, use safer alternatives such as `strftime` or `localtime_r` (in POSIX systems) which do not have this vulnerability. Always ensure that you are using thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `ctime`, you can use `strftime` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    std::time_t t = std::time(nullptr);\n    char mbstr[100];\n    if (std::strftime(mbstr, sizeof(mbstr), \"%A %c\", std::localtime(&t))) {\n        std::cout << mbstr << '\\n';\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `strftime` is used to format the time into a string. The `localtime` function is used to convert the time_t value to a struct tm, which `strftime` can use.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- ctime\n- iostream\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5bce7e99-0e71-4814-8eb9-ed9cb4c826ed",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the function `fprintf` is used, which can potentially lead to format string vulnerabilities if user-controlled input is passed as the format string argument.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that include proper bounds checking or that are not susceptible to format string vulnerabilities. In the case of `fprintf`, a safer alternative could be `fputs` or `fwrite`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nfputs(\"[ERROR]: reg_state file not found\\n\", stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `stdio.h`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cff4ac57-6dc2-4790-a12a-59ab2b9b1714",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strlen((*kv)`, the `strlen` function is called on a dereferenced pointer `kv`. If `kv` points to a non-null-terminated string, or if it is a null pointer, this can lead to undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, you should check that pointers are not null before dereferencing them.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (kv != nullptr && *kv != nullptr) {\n    size_t length = strlen(*kv);\n    // ...\n} else {\n    // Handle error...\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check that `kv` is not a null pointer, and that it does not point to a null pointer, before calling `strlen`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021",
                  "cff4ac57-6dc2-4790-a12a-59ab2b9b1714"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "45a63201-085c-4a36-81aa-d72d57867a76",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, leading to potential security risks such as code execution, data corruption, and denial of service. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the `fname` parameter is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using `fstream` objects from the C++ Standard Library, which provide a higher level of type safety and are less prone to errors.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` object from the C++ Standard Library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream infile;\ninfile.open(fname, std::ios::binary);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This library provides facilities for file-based input and output.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cff4ac57-6dc2-4790-a12a-59ab2b9b1714",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strlen((*kv)`, the `strlen` function is called on a dereferenced pointer `kv`. If `kv` points to a non-null-terminated string, or if it is a null pointer, this can lead to undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, you should check that pointers are not null before dereferencing them.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (kv != nullptr && *kv != nullptr) {\n    size_t length = strlen(*kv);\n    // ...\n} else {\n    // Handle error...\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check that `kv` is not a null pointer, and that it does not point to a null pointer, before calling `strlen`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5c08f68-e704-4635-9dc4-d9dd9ebff256",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflow vulnerabilities. One such function is `sprintf`.\n\nThe `sprintf` function is used to write formatted data to a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause crashes, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `sprintf` and other similar functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do perform bounds checking, such as `snprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `sprintf` with `snprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe code\nchar buffer[50];\nsprintf(buffer, \"%s\", veryLongString);\n\n// Safe code\nchar buffer[50];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", veryLongString);\n```\n\nIn the safe code, `snprintf` will write at most `sizeof(buffer)` characters to `buffer`, including the null terminator. This ensures that `buffer` will not overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sprintf` and `snprintf` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189",
                  "b5c08f68-e704-4635-9dc4-d9dd9ebff256"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5c08f68-e704-4635-9dc4-d9dd9ebff256",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflow vulnerabilities. One such function is `sprintf`.\n\nThe `sprintf` function is used to write formatted data to a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause crashes, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `sprintf` and other similar functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do perform bounds checking, such as `snprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `sprintf` with `snprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe code\nchar buffer[50];\nsprintf(buffer, \"%s\", veryLongString);\n\n// Safe code\nchar buffer[50];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", veryLongString);\n```\n\nIn the safe code, `snprintf` will write at most `sizeof(buffer)` characters to `buffer`, including the null terminator. This ensures that `buffer` will not overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sprintf` and `snprintf` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
