            {
              "id": "b5c08f68-e704-4635-9dc4-d9dd9ebff256",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflow vulnerabilities. One such function is `sprintf`.\n\nThe `sprintf` function is used to write formatted data to a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause crashes, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `sprintf` and other similar functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do perform bounds checking, such as `snprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `sprintf` with `snprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe code\nchar buffer[50];\nsprintf(buffer, \"%s\", veryLongString);\n\n// Safe code\nchar buffer[50];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", veryLongString);\n```\n\nIn the safe code, `snprintf` will write at most `sizeof(buffer)` characters to `buffer`, including the null terminator. This ensures that `buffer` will not overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sprintf` and `snprintf` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
