            {
              "id": "60ac0b5e-fa8c-473a-bd44-9f910ed452b6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C library functions that are considered unsafe. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to serious vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. \n\nIn this case, the function `rewind` is considered as a vulnerability sink. The `rewind` function sets the file position to the beginning of the file for the stream pointed to by stream. It also clears the error and end-of-file indicators for the stream.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of using `rewind`, consider using `fseek` or `fsetpos`. These functions provide more control over error handling and do not clear the error indicator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nFILE* file = fopen(\"file.txt\", \"r\");\n// some operations on the file\nrewind(file);\n```\n\nConsider using:\n\n```cpp\nFILE* file = fopen(\"file.txt\", \"r\");\n// some operations on the file\nfseek(file, 0, SEEK_SET);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdio.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fb107ecb-08bb-4866-b684-6e9316d02a03",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary runtime checks, and can lead to undefined behavior if used improperly.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `fprintf` is used, which is generally considered safe. However, if user-controlled input is passed to the format string parameter, it can lead to a format string vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using C standard library functions that do not perform bounds checking or other necessary runtime checks. Instead, use safer alternatives that are provided by the C++ standard library or other libraries that provide the necessary checks.\n\nFor the `fprintf` function, if user-controlled input is passed to the format string parameter, ensure that the input is properly sanitized and validated before use. Alternatively, avoid using user-controlled input in the format string parameter altogether.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `fprintf` function is used safely as no user-controlled input is passed to the format string parameter. However, if user-controlled input is to be included in the error message, use the `%s` format specifier and pass the input as an argument to `fprintf`, like so:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = /* user-controlled input */;\nfprintf(stderr, \"[ERROR] Invalid buffer as read length was exceeded: %s\\n\", user_input);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `stdio.h`: for the `fprintf` function and `stderr` file pointer.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a616b0e0-3b77-4923-975b-8e80bf4ab513",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `fputs` or `fwrite`. These functions do not interpret the format string, reducing the risk of format string vulnerabilities. Additionally, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fprintf` function with `fputs`:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[50];\nsprintf(buffer, \"Metrics version %d not supported\\n\", pmmetrics_version);\nfputs(buffer, stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- stdio.h: for `fprintf`, `fputs`, and `sprintf` functions\n- stdlib.h: for `exit` function\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "33a821d2-e0d9-4fa3-a74d-3434b09d3aaf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `fprintf` is used, which can potentially lead to format string vulnerabilities if user-controlled input is passed as the format string argument.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions whenever possible. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library or other libraries that perform bounds checking or that properly handle format strings. \n\nIn the case of `fprintf`, if you're only using it to print constant strings or strings that don't contain format specifiers (`%s`, `%d`, etc.), it's generally safe. However, if you're using it to print user-controlled input or strings that may contain format specifiers, consider using other functions that don't interpret format specifiers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, since the string being printed doesn't contain any format specifiers, the `fprintf` function is safe. However, to avoid potential issues in the future, you could replace it with `fputs`:\n\n```cpp\nfputs(\"[ERROR]: pm_metrics file not found \\n\", stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `stdio.h`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
