            {
              "id": "45a63201-085c-4a36-81aa-d72d57867a76",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, leading to potential security risks such as code execution, data corruption, and denial of service. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the `fname` parameter is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using `fstream` objects from the C++ Standard Library, which provide a higher level of type safety and are less prone to errors.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` object from the C++ Standard Library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream infile;\ninfile.open(fname, std::ios::binary);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This library provides facilities for file-based input and output.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3857df69-7bcc-44e8-8c0b-cc2a9b733a16",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be insecure because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking to prevent buffer overflow. In C++, the `std::snprintf` function can be used as a safer alternative to `sprintf`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::snprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name, sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name), \"%s[%d]\", table[x].field_name, y);\n```\n\nIn this fix, `std::snprintf` is used instead of `sprintf`. The second argument to `std::snprintf` is the size of the destination buffer, which ensures that the function does not write past the end of the buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: for `std::snprintf`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the provided links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d620a8e6-d31d-45e9-8c3c-513df442b7c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which does not check the size of the destination buffer and can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `sprintf` such as `snprintf`, which includes a parameter for the maximum number of bytes to be written to the destination buffer. This can prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that the destination buffer is not written beyond its size.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name, sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name), \"%s\", table[x].field_name);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for the `snprintf` function\n- `stdlib.h` for dynamic memory allocation functions if used elsewhere in the code\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b35da78e-60d4-4a63-8994-c5c27affdc12",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and integer overflows. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fprintf`, you can use `fputs` or `fwrite` which do not interpret the format string. Also, always validate and sanitize input data before processing it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n\n// ...\n\nfputs(\"[ERROR]: Invalid buffer as buffer length exceeded\\n\", stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstdio>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c5a3bea3-3cbf-4546-94a7-e02f67fe7adf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In this case, the function `std::strerror` is used, which is not thread-safe and can cause data races in multi-threaded programs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions like `std::strerror`. Instead, use alternatives that are thread-safe and less prone to errors. In C++, you can use `std::error_code` and `std::error_condition` which are part of the `<system_error>` library. These classes are designed to hold and transport error conditions, which are portable across different systems.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `std::strerror` function with `std::error_code` and `std::error_condition`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <system_error>\n\nstd::error_code ec (ENOENT, std::system_category());\nstd::cout << ec.message() << std::endl;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<system_error>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e44f810b-0c1d-4249-a0e4-06af9d2fb12f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe or insecure. In this case, the function `std::strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions or other unpredictable behavior.\n\nThe `std::strerror(errno)` function returns a pointer to a string that describes the error code passed in the argument errno. The string is actually a static buffer that is shared by all threads, so if another thread calls `std::strerror(errno)` before the first thread has finished using the string, the result is undefined.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `std::strerror(errno)`. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives such as `strerror_r()` (in POSIX) or `strerror_s()` (in Windows). These functions store the error message in a user-provided buffer, so they don't have the thread-safety issues of `std::strerror(errno)`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `std::strerror(errno)` with `strerror_r()` or `strerror_s()`. Here is an example of how to use `strerror_r()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif (strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n    // Use buf...\n} else {\n    // Handle error...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<string.h>`: for `strerror_r()`\n- `<errno.h>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "022af024-c19e-4382-98f8-df7ec18f7dd1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path (`sysfs_path`) is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to the `fopen` function. In C++, the `fstream` library provides the `ifstream` and `ofstream` classes that can be used to read and write files, respectively. These classes provide a higher level of abstraction and are less prone to security issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` class from the `fstream` library. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(sysfs_path.c_str(), std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This library provides facilities for file-based input and output.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ddff8fdb-af8d-46f3-bfcd-c6127bc31cd2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `std::getenv(name)` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is not found, the function returns a null pointer. This can lead to null pointer dereferencing if not properly checked.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. Always validate and sanitize input data. Be aware of the potential security risks associated with the functions you are using.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv(name)`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::getenv` function in combination with checks to prevent null pointer dereferencing:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* value = std::getenv(name);\nif (value == nullptr) {\n    // Handle the error\n} else {\n    s = value;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header defines several general purpose functions, including dynamic memory management, random number generation, communication with the environment, integer arithmetics, searching, sorting and converting.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-628: Function Call with Incorrectly Specified Arguments](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/628.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e44f810b-0c1d-4249-a0e4-06af9d2fb12f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe or insecure. In this case, the function `std::strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions or other unpredictable behavior.\n\nThe `std::strerror(errno)` function returns a pointer to a string that describes the error code passed in the argument errno. The string is actually a static buffer that is shared by all threads, so if another thread calls `std::strerror(errno)` before the first thread has finished using the string, the result is undefined.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `std::strerror(errno)`. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives such as `strerror_r()` (in POSIX) or `strerror_s()` (in Windows). These functions store the error message in a user-provided buffer, so they don't have the thread-safety issues of `std::strerror(errno)`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `std::strerror(errno)` with `strerror_r()` or `strerror_s()`. Here is an example of how to use `strerror_r()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif (strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n    // Use buf...\n} else {\n    // Handle error...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<string.h>`: for `strerror_r()`\n- `<errno.h>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
