            {
              "id": "c5a3bea3-3cbf-4546-94a7-e02f67fe7adf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In this case, the function `std::strerror` is used, which is not thread-safe and can cause data races in multi-threaded programs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions like `std::strerror`. Instead, use alternatives that are thread-safe and less prone to errors. In C++, you can use `std::error_code` and `std::error_condition` which are part of the `<system_error>` library. These classes are designed to hold and transport error conditions, which are portable across different systems.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `std::strerror` function with `std::error_code` and `std::error_condition`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <system_error>\n\nstd::error_code ec (ENOENT, std::system_category());\nstd::cout << ec.message() << std::endl;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<system_error>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
