            {
              "id": "dd292675-5e51-4634-b094-8772f6e22b51",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `std::localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded context. In C++, prefer using thread-safe alternatives provided by the language standard library. For instance, instead of `std::localtime`, use `std::localtime_s` or `std::get_time`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix using `std::localtime_s`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\nstd::time_t timer;\nstd::tm bt = {};\nstd::localtime_s(&bt, &timer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<ctime>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f5d99ff3-dbc2-4cd3-a7c1-93deadf7dccd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often referred to as \"banned\" or \"prohibited\" functions. The `ctime` function is one such function. It converts a time_t value into a string, but it uses a static buffer that can be overwritten by subsequent calls, leading to potential data corruption or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `ctime` function. Instead, use safer alternatives such as `strftime` or `localtime_r` (in POSIX systems) which do not have this vulnerability. Always ensure that you are using thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `ctime`, you can use `strftime` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    std::time_t t = std::time(nullptr);\n    char mbstr[100];\n    if (std::strftime(mbstr, sizeof(mbstr), \"%A %c\", std::localtime(&t))) {\n        std::cout << mbstr << '\\n';\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `strftime` is used to format the time into a string. The `localtime` function is used to convert the time_t value to a struct tm, which `strftime` can use.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- ctime\n- iostream\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8287dc6a-3fca-45ea-9b6b-2794d6fb54e7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and integer overflows. In the provided code snippet, the function `sscanf()` is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly validated or sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy()` instead of `strcpy()`, `snprintf()` instead of `sprintf()`, and `fgets()` instead of `gets()`. Also, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `sscanf()` function is used to read formatted input from a string. The second argument `%s` can lead to buffer overflow if the input string is longer than the buffer size. To fix this, you can specify a maximum width for the string to prevent buffer overflow:\n\n```cpp\nrc = sscanf(message, \"%x %99s\", &pid, task);\n```\n\nIn this case, `99` is the maximum width, which should be one less than the size of the buffer `task` to leave room for the null terminator.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2ea8704a-7bc7-49bd-a1a1-863d7d658279",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the function in question is `sscanf`.\n\nThe `sscanf` function is used to read formatted input from a string. However, it does not perform bounds checking, which means it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the input is not properly controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `sscanf` and other unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or ensure that you manually perform bounds checking before using these functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `sscanf`, you can use `strncpy` or `snprintf` which are safer as they take the size of the buffer as an argument, preventing buffer overflows.\n\nHere is an example of how you can replace `sscanf` with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nchar dest[50];\nchar src[50] = \"hello world\";\nstrncpy(dest, src, sizeof(dest));\ndest[sizeof(dest) - 1] = 0; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sscanf` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6330ccde-f58d-4201-845b-779c35864e57",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a counted buffer operation (such as strncpy). When the source and destination memory regions overlap, the behavior of functions like strncpy is undefined, which can lead to unpredictable results, data corruption, or security vulnerabilities. The classification indicates a high-confidence issue. There is no evidence in the available data that the source and destination are guaranteed to be non-overlapping, and no mitigating factors (such as explicit checks or use of non-overlapping buffers) are present. Therefore, this is considered a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior caused by possible overlapping source and destination buffers in functions like strncpy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, consider using a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as memmove, or restructure the code to guarantee non-overlapping buffers. For example, add a check to verify that the source and destination do not overlap:\n\n```cpp\nif ((dst + count <= src) || (src + count <= dst)) {\n    strncpy(dst, src, count);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use memmove or copy to a temporary buffer\n    memmove(dst, src, count);\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, refactor the code to ensure that the source and destination are always distinct buffers.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2ea8704a-7bc7-49bd-a1a1-863d7d658279",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the function in question is `sscanf`.\n\nThe `sscanf` function is used to read formatted input from a string. However, it does not perform bounds checking, which means it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the input is not properly controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `sscanf` and other unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or ensure that you manually perform bounds checking before using these functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `sscanf`, you can use `strncpy` or `snprintf` which are safer as they take the size of the buffer as an argument, preventing buffer overflows.\n\nHere is an example of how you can replace `sscanf` with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nchar dest[50];\nchar src[50] = \"hello world\";\nstrncpy(dest, src, sizeof(dest));\ndest[sizeof(dest) - 1] = 0; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `sscanf` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
