            {
              "id": "58c02fca-ab1c-4649-b91c-e81dfb243b4e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a string copy operation. When the source and destination memory regions overlap, functions like strncpy can produce unpredictable results, including data corruption or crashes. The classification specifically mentions undefined behavior due to possible overlap, which is a well-known risk in C/C++ buffer operations. There is no evidence in the available information that the source and destination are guaranteed to be non-overlapping, and no mitigating factors such as explicit checks or guards are present. Therefore, this is considered a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed to ensure memory safety.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying strings, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Before performing the copy, add a check to verify that the memory regions are distinct. If overlap is possible, consider using a temporary buffer to safely copy the data in two steps:\n\n```cpp\nif (dst != src) {\n    strncpy(dst, src, count);\n} else {\n    // If overlap is possible, use a temporary buffer\n    char temp_buffer[MAX_SIZE]; // Ensure MAX_SIZE is large enough\n    memcpy(temp_buffer, src, count);\n    memcpy(dst, temp_buffer, count);\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you know the regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(dst, src, count);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
