              "id": "ea467089-6b0d-45d0-941f-e5d94dae46aa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`, or `snprintf` instead of `sprintf`. In the case of `sscanf`, it is recommended to use a combination of other functions like `strtok` and `strtol` to parse the input string in a safer way.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative to the provided code:\n\n```cpp\nchar *token;\nchar *endptr;\nlong value;\n\ntoken = strtok(message, \" \");\nif (token != NULL) {\n    value = strtol(token, &endptr, 16);\n    if (*endptr == '\\0') {\n        reset_seq_num = value;\n        token = strtok(NULL, \"\\n\");\n        if (token != NULL) {\n            strncpy(tmp, token, sizeof(tmp) - 1);\n            tmp[sizeof(tmp) - 1] = '\\0';\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis code first tokenizes the input string by space, then converts the first token to a long integer using `strtol`. It then checks if the conversion was successful by checking if `*endptr` is a null character. If it is, it assigns the converted value to `reset_seq_num`, then tokenizes the remaining string by newline and copies it to `tmp` using `strncpy`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `#include <stdio.h>` for `sscanf`\n- `#include <string.h>` for `strtok` and `strncpy`\n- `#include <stdlib.h>` for `strtol`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
