            {
              "id": "bde1afd8-8837-4eb1-97a8-4c8e66299290",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`, `snprintf` instead of `sprintf`, etc. Also, always validate and sanitize user inputs to prevent any malicious data from causing harm.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `sscanf` function is used to parse a string. The problem here is that the `%s` format specifier does not limit the number of characters to be read, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input string is longer than the buffer size. A safer alternative would be to use the `%ns` format specifier, where `n` is the maximum number of characters to be read.\n\n```cpp\n#define MAX_TASK_NAME 256\nchar task_name[MAX_TASK_NAME];\nsscanf(message, \"%x:%255s\\n\", &pid, task_name);\n```\n\nIn this case, `255` is used instead of `256` to leave room for the null terminator.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1786e313-b10a-4cac-8246-d475b2924c97",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`, `snprintf` instead of `sprintf`, etc. Also, always validate and control the input before processing it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, you can use `strtol` function to convert the string to an integer and `strncpy` to copy the string. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar* event_in = /*...*/;\nunsigned int event;\nchar message[256];\n\nchar* endptr;\nevent = strtol(event_in, &endptr, 16);\nif (endptr != event_in) {\n    strncpy(message, endptr, sizeof(message) - 1);\n    message[sizeof(message) - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- cstring\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dadad5de-5548-4e68-a07c-38b7062853a1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(schema_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability if `schema_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter to prevent buffer overflows, such as `strnlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure schema_key is null-terminated\nschema_key[schema_key.size()] = '\\0';\n\n// Use strnlen instead of strlen\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(schema_key, schema_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and `std::string::substr`\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen` and `std::strnlen`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e69ad5e-765c-4da7-8cac-de056af136d8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to determine the length of a string, but it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. This is because `strlen` does not include the null terminator in its count, and if the string is not properly null-terminated, `strlen` can read past the end of the buffer, leading to a buffer overflow.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(version_key)` is used as an argument to the `substr` function. If `version_key` is not a null-terminated string, this could potentially lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using `strlen` where possible, and instead use safer alternatives that do not risk buffer overflow. In C++, this could be the `std::string::length` or `std::string::size` functions, which are safe to use as they do not read past the end of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strlen` function with `std::string::length` or `std::string::size`. Here is how the fixed code might look:\n\n```cpp\nfeature_line.substr(version_key.length()).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: This is a standard C++ library for using string objects.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b626a0bb-2ae3-4ffc-b11f-eb901d18dda6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be insecure because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use secure alternatives to `sprintf` that perform bounds checking, such as `snprintf`. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string including the null-terminating character. Avoid using functions that do not perform bounds checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` and ensure that the size argument does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(str + 23 + 5, sizeof(str) - 28, \"%08x\", uuid->asic_0);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
