            {
              "id": "dc244db5-f771-48e8-afa1-0fc74cbf3344",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and integer overflows. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be insecure because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking to prevent buffer overflow. In C++, the `std::snprintf` function can be used as a safer alternative to `sprintf`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `std::snprintf` function:\n\n```cpp\nstd::snprintf(str + 19 + 5, sizeof(str) - 24, \"%04x\", uuid->asic_4);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: for `std::snprintf` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
