              "id": "80366015-e36e-4301-a033-e40ca1b0fb93",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C functions in a C++ program. These functions are considered unsafe because they do not perform bounds checking, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer and can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is too large.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that perform bounds checking. For `sprintf`, a safer alternative is `snprintf`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of bytes to be written to the destination buffer, including the null-terminating character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(str + 13, sizeof(str) - 13, \"-\");\n```\n\nThis will ensure that no more than `sizeof(str) - 13` bytes are written to `str + 13`, preventing a buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
