            {
              "id": "310f6457-027a-45a5-8770-4e2a71113cf6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are considered insecure because they do not perform bounds checking and can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In the provided code snippet, the `sprintf` function is used, which is one of these insecure functions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these insecure functions. In the case of `sprintf`, a safer alternative would be `snprintf`, which includes a parameter for the maximum number of bytes to be written to the output string, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(str + 9, sizeof(str) - 9, \"%04x\", uuid->time_mid);\n```\n\nIn this version, `snprintf` is used instead of `sprintf`, and the maximum number of bytes to be written is specified as `sizeof(str) - 9`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: for the `snprintf` function\n- `<cstddef>`: for the `sizeof` operator\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the provided links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
