            {
              "id": "8956aab6-8854-490d-924a-a3e23764852e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In C++, it's recommended to use safer alternatives provided by the language.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `sprintf()` is used, which is a function known for its potential to cause buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This function does not check the bounds of the buffer and can write more data than the buffer can hold, leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it's recommended to use safer alternatives to `sprintf()`. In C++, you can use `snprintf()`, which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be written to the buffer, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(str + 8, sizeof(str) - 8, \"-\");\n```\n\nIn this version, `snprintf()` is used instead of `sprintf()`. The second argument to `snprintf()` is the maximum number of characters to be written to the buffer, which is calculated as the size of the buffer minus the offset.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
