            {
              "id": "528831f5-971c-4cd2-b262-f08e8d880d16",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard C library function that converts a string into an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not handle errors well. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use functions that can handle errors better, such as `strtol()`, `sscanf()`, or the C++ `std::stoi()` function. These functions provide mechanisms to check if the entire string was converted, if any characters were left over, or if the converted value was out of range.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi()` with `std::stoi()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\ntry {\n    ret = std::stoi(ev_str);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n    // handle error: the string was not a valid integer\n} catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n    // handle error: the integer was out of range for int\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
