              "id": "db0fe09d-a425-4920-9a64-93d383b99596",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For instance, instead of `sscanf`, you can use `std::istringstream` along with `std::getline` or `std::istream::operator>>`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::istringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string fd_bdf_str;\nstd::istringstream iss(bdfline);\nif (!(iss >> fd_bdf_str)) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
