            {
              "id": "561a04ef-b2b9-4480-91ec-ee341dc0d93b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or restricted.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fscanf`, you can use `fgets` and `sscanf` which are safer as they allow you to specify a maximum buffer size.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative to the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\n#define BUFF_SIZE 256\n\ndouble pcie_speed;\nchar buff[BUFF_SIZE];\n\nif (fgets(buff, sizeof(buff), fp) != NULL) {\n    sscanf(buff, \"%lf %s\", &pcie_speed, buff);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
