            {
              "id": "bfd9b0fc-efec-42a1-a04f-28089214aeeb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a counted buffer operation (such as strncpy). When the source and destination memory regions overlap, the behavior of functions like strncpy is undefined according to the C standard. This can lead to unpredictable results, data corruption, or security vulnerabilities. The classification indicates a high-confidence issue, and there are no signals in the available data to suggest that this is a false positive. Overlap risks are particularly serious because they may not be caught during testing and can result in subtle bugs or exploitable conditions in production.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior caused by overlapping source and destination buffers in a buffer copy operation (such as strncpy), ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as memmove, or refactor the code to avoid overlap entirely. For example, if you are using strncpy and there is a chance that the source and destination buffers may overlap, replace the call with memmove, which is defined to handle overlapping memory safely. Always validate the pointers and ensure that the memory regions are distinct before copying.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1f6a3403-844b-413d-a944-1bc8f9741821",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a counted buffer operation (such as strncpy). When the source and destination memory regions overlap, functions like strncpy can exhibit undefined behavior, potentially leading to data corruption or security vulnerabilities. The classification indicates a high-confidence issue, and there is no evidence in the available context to suggest that the source and destination are guaranteed to be non-overlapping. Therefore, this is considered a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a function that is safe for overlapping memory, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. For example, replace the original copy operation with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(destination, source, count);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy is performed safely even if the source and destination regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "83e0f15a-afe7-4efe-82bc-2fc1ac3a04f1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "The finding is classified as a potential undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a string copy operation. However, the input does not provide enough contextual information about the actual code, such as the specific buffer expressions, types, or whether overlap is truly feasible. In the absence of concrete evidence of overlapping memory regions, this type of finding is often a false positive, especially when the source is a string literal or a constant string, which cannot overlap with the destination. Therefore, while the classification indicates a risk, the lack of supporting details makes it unlikely to be a genuine vulnerability.\n\n___\n"
