            {
              "id": "71290303-4393-4f4e-8ff8-c3ddb9049d47",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\nThe `sscanf` function is used to read formatted input from a string. If the string contains malicious data, it can lead to unexpected behavior or security vulnerabilities. In this case, the function is used to read four hexadecimal numbers from a string. If the string contains more than four numbers, or if the numbers are larger than expected, it can lead to buffer overflows or other types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using the `sscanf` function, or any other function that can lead to buffer overflows or format string vulnerabilities. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or that are not susceptible to format string vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `sscanf` is to use the `std::istringstream` class from the C++ Standard Library. Here is how you can rewrite the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n#include <iomanip>\n\nstd::istringstream iss(bdf_str);\niss >> std::hex >> domain >> bus >> device >> function;\nif (!iss || iss.rdbuf()->in_avail() != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nThis code reads four hexadecimal numbers from the string, just like the original code. However, it does not suffer from the same vulnerabilities as the `sscanf` function.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n- `<iomanip>`: for `std::hex`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
