              "id": "c4f27189-f833-4100-b6ae-cfed67a2cf3f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "The finding is classified as a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a string copy operation. This is a well-known issue with functions like `strncpy`, which do not guarantee correct behavior if the source and destination memory regions overlap. The classification indicates a high-confidence, genuine vulnerability. There is no evidence in the available data to suggest that the source and destination are guaranteed to be non-overlapping, and the risk is inherent to the use of these functions. Overlapping memory regions during such operations can lead to unpredictable results, data corruption, or crashes. This type of issue is a common source of subtle bugs and security vulnerabilities in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy` or similar functions. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the vulnerable string copy operation with `memmove`, ensuring that the size argument does not exceed the destination buffer's capacity.\n\n```cpp\n// Example fix:\nmemmove(destination, source, count);\n```\n\nMake sure that `count` does not exceed the size of the destination buffer to avoid buffer overflows. Always validate the pointers and the length before performing the operation.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
