            {
              "id": "a79544ec-d573-4c7b-b103-83f03712e312",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "The finding is classified as a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a string copy operation. This type of issue can lead to unpredictable program behavior, data corruption, or security vulnerabilities. The classification specifically mentions the risk of undefined behavior with strncpy when the source and destination may overlap. There is no evidence in the available information to suggest that the source and destination are guaranteed to be non-overlapping, and no mitigating factors such as explicit checks or guards are present. Therefore, this is considered a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying strings, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling the string copy function. If there is any possibility of overlap, consider using a memory-safe function or explicitly check for overlap and handle accordingly. For example, you can add a check before the copy operation:\n\n```c\nif (dst != src) {\n    // safe to use strncpy\n    strncpy(dst, src, count);\n} else {\n    // handle overlap case, possibly with memmove or by skipping the copy\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if overlap is possible and unavoidable, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping memory regions:\n\n```c\nmemmove(dst, src, count);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5ef9b91d-6a10-4ab3-8621-023ce936a45b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as high risk because the destination buffer's capacity is not known, and the count or length argument used in the buffer operation is not proven to be safe. This means there is a significant risk of a buffer overflow, which can lead to security vulnerabilities such as memory corruption or arbitrary code execution. The lack of information about the destination buffer's size and the absence of a proven safe count make it difficult to guarantee that the operation will not write past the end of the buffer. No evidence of safety checks or guards is present, and there is no indication that the count is derived from the buffer's actual capacity. As a result, this is considered a genuine vulnerability that requires remediation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate this buffer overflow risk, always ensure that the destination buffer's size is known and that the count or length argument used in the buffer operation does not exceed this size. If the destination is a pointer, pass an additional parameter specifying its capacity, and use this value to limit the number of bytes written. For example, if copying data into a buffer, use a function like `snprintf` or `strncpy` with the buffer's size as the maximum count, and always ensure the buffer is properly null-terminated if working with strings. If the buffer's size cannot be determined, refactor the code to make it explicit and enforce bounds checking before performing the operation.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
