                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, application crashes, and other serious security issues. The function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_2)` in the provided code might be using such insecure C functions internally.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using insecure C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For instance, instead of using `strcpy()`, use `strncpy()`. Similarly, instead of `sprintf()`, use `snprintf()`. Always ensure that you are not writing more data to a buffer than it can hold.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the actual implementation of `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_2)`, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using insecure C functions, they should be replaced with their safer counterparts. For example, if the function is using `strcpy()`, it should be replaced with `strncpy()`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout the complete code, it's hard to list all library dependencies. However, if the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_2)` is part of a library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
